{
  "description": "Issue #830, execution twin of `heritage/check/heritage-value-of-key-reader-in-parentheses`. `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules and the parenthesized reader must be proven to RUN, not merely to parse clean. Every print pins a VALUE that exists only if the `(` path actually entered the `value-of-reader` (`spec/grammar.md:203`, defined :217) instead of taking `value` as a `parenthesized-call` callee (:215) — the defect #830 reported, fixed incidentally by #885 and pinned here. Covers the reader wholly parenthesized, as its own dictionary operand, as its own key operand (the inner reader reports the word \"a\", which the outer then uses as its key), redundantly grouped, composed with infix `+`, and carrying a `.field` postfix (proving it is a real `primary`, :192); the last two prints show the parenthesized reader agreeing with the Core `:d[\"a\"]`/`:nested.inner.x` twins it corresponds to. The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:301`).",
